# apogee-sq521 configuration.
#
# Copy to /etc/apogee-sq521/config.env, mode 0640, owner root:daniel:
#
#   sudo install -d -m 0755 /etc/apogee-sq521
#   sudo install -m 0640 -o root -g daniel config.example.env /etc/apogee-sq521/config.env
#   sudoedit /etc/apogee-sq521/config.env      # fill in MQTT_PASSWORD
#
# Then check it — through systemd, so the file is parsed exactly as the unit
# will parse it — before starting anything:
#
#   sudo systemd-run --pty --quiet --collect --uid=daniel \
#       --property=EnvironmentFile=/etc/apogee-sq521/config.env \
#       --property=StateDirectory=apogee-sq521 \
#       /usr/local/bin/apogee-sq521 --check-config
#
# Every variable below is optional and is shown at its built-in default, so a
# file containing nothing but MQTT_PASSWORD is a complete, working config.
# Uncomment a line only to change it — the daemon prints the resolved values,
# whatever their source, in `--check-config` and in its startup log line.
#
# systemd EnvironmentFile syntax, which is NOT shell:
#   * The file must be UTF-8. PPFD_UNIT below is non-ASCII (µ and ²) and will
#     be mangled or rejected if this file is saved as latin-1.
#   * No `export`, no `$VAR` expansion, no command substitution.
#   * Leading/trailing whitespace is stripped; interior whitespace is kept.
#   * In an unquoted value a backslash escapes the next character, so a literal
#     backslash must be doubled. Single-quote anything with punctuation in it
#     (see MQTT_PASSWORD) and the whole value is taken verbatim.


# --- Identity ---------------------------------------------------------------

# NODE_ID is this device's name on the bus. It is an MQTT topic segment AND an
# InfluxDB tag in grow-history-recorder, so changing it on a live install
# orphans every point of history recorded under the old name. It also names the
# single-instance lock (@apogee-sq521-<node>), which is what lets a second
# publisher on this Pi run without fighting this one over the sensor.
# Change it only if this Pi ever hosts a second SQ-521.
#NODE_ID=quantum-sensor

# MQTT_TOPIC_PREFIX is the site root. Same warning as NODE_ID: grow-app keys
# history on these exact strings. Discovery goes to <prefix>/_discovery.
#MQTT_TOPIC_PREFIX=grow/daniel-home


# --- Broker -----------------------------------------------------------------

# The Mosquitto instance in media-stack. An IP literal rather than a name so a
# DNS outage cannot take the sensor off the bus.
#MQTT_HOST=192.168.8.3
#MQTT_PORT=1883

# The shared edge account. Change only if the broker's ACLs are reworked.
#MQTT_USERNAME=edge-daniel-home

# REQUIRED — the daemon refuses to start without it (exit 78).
#
# This is the MQTT_EDGE_PASSWORD value from the media-stack deployment. Fetch it
# from there; it is not in this repo and never should be.
#
# Anonymous access is not supported: the Python publisher connected with an empty
# password, paho retried the failing CONNECT for ever on its network thread while
# publish() returned success into a queue, and nothing ever reached the bus. The
# logs looked healthy. Refusing to start is the fix.
#
# Single-quote it if it contains a backslash, quote or other punctuation:
#   MQTT_PASSWORD='pa$$w\rd'
#
# To read the value back later — every mosquitto_pub/mosquitto_sub in the README
# needs it — let systemd parse the file rather than sourcing it, because the
# syntax above is not shell:
#
#   export MQTT_PASSWORD="$(sudo systemd-run --pipe --quiet --collect \
#       --property=EnvironmentFile=/etc/apogee-sq521/config.env \
#       /usr/bin/printenv MQTT_PASSWORD)"
MQTT_PASSWORD=


# --- Sensor -----------------------------------------------------------------

# The Dr. Liu SDI-12-to-USB adapter (FTDI FT231X). The by-id path, not
# /dev/ttyUSB0: the Pi hosts other USB serial devices and enumeration order is
# not stable across reboots. Change it only if the adapter is replaced — the
# serial number D30GQUR5 is baked into the path. Find the new one with
# `ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/`.
#SDI12_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT231X_USB_UART_D30GQUR5-if00-port0

# The sensor's SDI-12 bus address, exactly one character from [0-9a-zA-Z].
# Every command is prefixed with it ("0M!", "0D0!"). Change it only if the
# sensor has been re-addressed with aAb!.
#SDI12_ADDRESS=0

# Seconds between measurement cycles. Raising it costs DLI resolution.
#
# Short intervals are cheaper than they look, and an earlier version of this
# comment warned against going below ~5 s on two grounds that do not survive
# contact with the code or the hardware:
#
#   * The DLI integrator is not calibrated against a fixed sample spacing. It
#     integrates the measured dt between consecutive samples (trapezoidal, see
#     internal/dli accrue), so a shorter interval is strictly more accurate.
#     Its one spacing-dependent constant is the 60 s max-gap guard, which a
#     shorter interval moves away from, not towards.
#   * The three sub-commands are not "back to back" at a short interval. A
#     healthy transaction finishes in milliseconds — the 33 s cycle budget is a
#     ceiling built from timeouts, not the sensor's conversion time.
#
# Measured on quantum-sensor at POLL_SECONDS=2: publishes land 2.00 s apart with
# no skipped boundaries and no warnings. Note OFFLINE_AFTER's worst case does
# NOT shorten with the interval — it is OFFLINE_AFTER x max(POLL_SECONDS, cycle
# budget), so it stays 1m39s until the interval exceeds 33 s.
#
# What a short interval does cost is history volume: every polled reading lands
# in InfluxDB via grow-history-recorder, so 10 s -> 2 s is 5x the points.
#POLL_SECONDS=10

# Per-operation serial timeouts, in seconds. These bound one read and one write,
# not a whole transaction — the cycle budget is derived from them (see
# --check-config). Raise SDI12_READ_TIMEOUT only if the journal shows framing
# errors on an otherwise healthy sensor.
#SDI12_READ_TIMEOUT=2
#SDI12_WRITE_TIMEOUT=1


# --- Publishing behaviour ---------------------------------------------------

# unit_of_measurement for the PPFD entity. NOTE: non-ASCII — "µmol/s/m²", with
# U+00B5 MICRO SIGN and U+00B2 SUPERSCRIPT TWO. systemd reads this file as UTF-8
# and rejects other encodings, so do not "fix" it to "umol/s/m2": the string
# lands in an InfluxDB tag and is part of the wire contract with grow-app.
#PPFD_UNIT=µmol/s/m²

# How many consecutive FAILED CYCLES before the retained availability turns
# "offline". One flaky read should not grey out the device card in grow-app.
#
# This is a count of cycles, not of seconds, and it is NOT
# OFFLINE_AFTER x POLL_SECONDS: a silent sensor spends the whole cycle budget
# (33 s at these defaults) before its cycle counts as failed, so the real worst
# case is OFFLINE_AFTER x max(POLL_SECONDS, cycle budget) = 1m39s. Both numbers
# are printed by --check-config and on the startup log line. See the README.
#OFFLINE_AFTER=3

# Seconds allowed for the graceful stop: retained "offline", broker disconnect,
# DLI flush. Raise it only if `systemctl stop` starts reporting an unclean
# teardown; TimeoutStopSec in the unit is set well above it.
#SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=3


# --- Substrate probes (METER TEROS) -----------------------------------------
#
# The TEROS probes share the Dr. Liu adapter with the quantum sensor, so they
# share this daemon too. That is not a convenience: one adapter is one file
# descriptor, and SDI-12 gives a sensor an unsolicited-transmit window between a
# measurement header and its data command, so two masters would collide on the
# wire even with a perfectly correct lock. Above the wire the probes are fully
# independent — their own MQTT device, publisher, retained state and failure
# ladder — so an unplugged probe never affects the PAR sensor or another probe.
#
# Comma-separated, each entry either a bare SDI-12 address or "address:node-id":
#
#   SUBSTRATE_PROBES=A                    -> address A, node id "substrate-a"
#   SUBSTRATE_PROBES=A,B,C,D              -> four probes, derived node ids
#   SUBSTRATE_PROBES=A:tent-1-pot-2       -> explicit node id
#
# Unset — the default — means no substrate hardware is fitted: no publisher is
# built and no extra address is polled, so the daemon behaves exactly as it did
# before this feature existed.
#
# Give probes LETTER addresses. The numeric range is deliberately kept clear as
# an arrivals area: METER ships probes on 0 (and this fleet has seen 1), so a
# factory-fresh probe added to a bus whose installed sensors sit on numbers can
# collide with one — and two devices at one address cannot be separated in
# software, because the change-address command is obeyed by both.
#
# A node id must use '-' and never '_': grow-app collapses '-' to '_' when
# deriving entity ids, so the two spellings would produce colliding ids for what
# are meant to be different devices. The daemon refuses to start on one.
#SUBSTRATE_PROBES=

# How many poll cycles pass between substrate reads.
#
# Water content moves over hours while PPFD moves over seconds, and every probe
# costs an SDI-12 transaction inside the PAR sensor's cycle budget. At the
# deployed POLL_SECONDS=2 the default 15 reads the probes every 30 s, which is
# far finer than a dryback needs and leaves the PAR cadence untouched.
#
# Raising it costs nothing but resolution on the substrate series; lowering it
# to 1 polls every probe on every PAR cycle, which is the setting to reach for
# when bench-testing a newly wired probe rather than for a running tent.
#SUBSTRATE_EVERY=15


# --- Not set here -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# STATE_DIRECTORY is supplied by systemd from StateDirectory=apogee-sq521 in the
# unit, as /var/lib/apogee-sq521. Setting it by hand here points the timezone
# override and the DLI state file somewhere systemd has not created and, under
# ProtectSystem=strict, has not made writable. Leave it alone.
